// Report what the directory said about a group AT GRANT TIME.
// If a grant names a group the lookup returns nothing for, saying so explicitly would
// have caught the `Domain Users` primary-group problem at the moment the grant was
// created.
//
// ⚠️ THE INTERESTING ANSWER IS A SUCCESSFUL ONE THAT FOUND NOBODY. `resolved:true,
// members:0` is not an error — the search worked — so nothing else on this page would ever
// mention it, and the operator would walk away believing they had granted a role to
// somebody. That is exactly the screen to avoid. A grant is never rolled back over this: the
// role IS granted, the directory just does not put anyone in it today.
function noteGrantedGroup(name, d) {
  if (!d) return;                                     // no directory configured — nothing to say
  if (!d.resolved)
    notify('"' + name + '" granted, but the directory could not be read: ' +
           (d.error || 'unknown error') + ' — membership will be retried.');
  else if (!d.members)
    notify('"' + name + '" granted, but the directory returned NO members for it. ' +
           'Check the group name.');
  else
    notify('"' + name + '" granted — ' + d.members + ' member(s).', 'info');
}
// Create a group by granting its first console role (no schema: the subject exists
// once it holds a role). More roles can be added later via its gear.
//
// It creates a GROUP and nothing else. `dn` was removed as a selector type — it was
// stored, replicated and read by nobody — so the button and this modal no longer offer it.
//
// There was a `dn` type here too, matching a client-certificate subject DN.
// It was ruled out: x509 certificates are about authentication, not authorization, and in
// the PKI model authorization lives externally rather than inside the certificate. When
// "users, groups and DNs" was read as keeping it, the clarification was that this means the
// user TABLE, not the certificate subject. So roles attach to rows in the DB, never to
// anything a certificate carries.
// With `dn` gone the Type control has one option left, so it goes too rather than
// becoming a select that cannot select.
